WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.120
Roald Dahl is one of the greatest children's&nbsp;
authors of all time — and plenty of movies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.600
been made from his books. But which adaptations&nbsp;
are worth a watch, and which are you better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.480
off skipping? From worst to best, these are&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes' rankings of Roald Dahl movies.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.800
So here's a fun fact for you: Quentin Tarantino&nbsp;
and Robert Rodriguez made a Roald Dahl movie —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.600
which sounds like it could be either the greatest&nbsp;
or worst thing in the world. And, well, turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.560
it was the worst. Before Tarantino and Rodriguez&nbsp;
teamed up for the anthology film Grindhouse, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.440
were two of the four directors who participated&nbsp;
in another anthology film: Four Rooms.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.720
Loosely based on the adult short fiction works of&nbsp;
Dahl, Four Rooms is about the myriad misadventures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.120
happening at a Hollywood hotel, with four separate&nbsp;
segments tied together by the new bellhop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.000
played by Tim Roth. While none of the directors'&nbsp;
four individual segments are particularly great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.720
Tarantino's is generally considered passable,&nbsp;
though not quite enough to pass muster with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:59.440
critics. Four Rooms received a 13% critics' score&nbsp;
and a 69% audience score on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.640
making it the worst movie on Tarantino's and&nbsp;
Rodriguez's filmographies — as well as Dahl's.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.040
The Witches was first adapted&nbsp;
to the big screen in 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.600
and then again 30 years later in 2020.&nbsp;
Well, not technically the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:20.000
as The Witches actually debuted as an HBO Max&nbsp;
original. Set in 1967, the film finds a young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.560
orphan boy and his grandmother running across&nbsp;
a convention of witches at a seaside hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.200
which is led by the world's Grand High Witch&nbsp;
— who, put simply, isn't a very nice woman.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:37.520
"I will reach into your chest and rip&nbsp;
out your withered, shrivelled heart."

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.400
Unfortunately, despite a wealth of&nbsp;
talent in front of and behind the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.400
The Witches crash-lands on its flying&nbsp;
broomstick. As one critic put it:

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.640
"Like far too many unnecessary remakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.520
The Witches doesn't do enough&nbsp;
to justify its existence."

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.240
And while The Witches might've&nbsp;
been meant for movie theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.920
the results were mediocre whatever the medium.&nbsp;
Actually, make that downright rotten — with 51%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.720
from critics and 42% from audiences&nbsp;
on Rotten Tomatoes. All in all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.640
it's not hard to imagine that Dahl would want&nbsp;
nothing to do with this particular misfire.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.320
Danny, the Champion of the World is a less&nbsp;
well-known Roald Dahl novel released in 1975,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:20.480
that also inspired a mostly unseen TV movie in&nbsp;
1989. The movie adaptation pairs Jeremy Irons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.680
with his son, Samuel Irons, who plays the titular&nbsp;
Danny. Together, the two star as a widowed father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.880
and son who concoct a plan to get revenge on a&nbsp;
snobby property developer. Danny, the Champion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.200
of the World isn't especially memorable,&nbsp;
though it did inspire one critic to say that:

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.160
"[It] resurrects a lost world of English life&nbsp;
and entertainment with winning charm and aplomb."

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.480
"One" being the operative word there, because&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes only has a single review for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.960
this movie on its site. As such, Danny, the&nbsp;
Champion of the World has no critics' score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.000
however, its audience score&nbsp;
is an underwhelming 64%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.400
Maybe if more people saw it, the score&nbsp;
would be higher, but as it stands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.000
Danny may be the champion of the world — but&nbsp;
he's not the champion of the Tomatometer.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.000
Released in 1982, Roahl Dahl's The BFG&nbsp;
is about a big, friendly giant — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.600
clue's in the name — who befriends a&nbsp;
precocious little girl named Sophie.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.280
Dahl's story was first adapted to the screen in&nbsp;
1989 as an animated feature. You'll be forgiven if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.840
you haven't seen it or even heard of it, though,&nbsp;
because pretty much nobody else has. The BFG only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.800
has two critical reviews, so it doesn't even have&nbsp;
an official Tomatometer score, though one critic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.160
praised it as a, quote, "giant tale of imagination&nbsp;
with a dark side," while another panned it as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.640
quote, "slight and unimpressive." So let's&nbsp;
call it a wash. The audience score is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.320
equally underwhelming 65% — which is still fresh,&nbsp;
but nothing to write home about! While The BFG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.320
would later go on to bigger and better things, the&nbsp;
1989 animated film was, alas, a big freaking dud.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.520
Esio Trot was Roald Dahl's last published&nbsp;
novel, and it's just the kind of love story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.840
you would expect Dahl to write — revolving&nbsp;
around a lonely old man named Mr. Hoppy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.200
a widow named Mrs. Silver,&nbsp;
and her pet tortoise, Alfie.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.520
Esio Trot was adapted into a BBC television film&nbsp;
in 2015, with Dustin Hoffman as Mr. Hoppy, Judi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.960
Dench as Mrs. Silver, and James Corden narrating.&nbsp;
This charming adaptation received plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.920
critical acclaim, but it wasn't widely seen across&nbsp;
the pond. Consequently, Esio Trot doesn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.480
an official critics' score on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;
though the one critic who reviewed it said it was:

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.400
"A decidedly daffy, rather ridiculously&nbsp;
resplendent reptilian elder romance. Don't ask."

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.520
Those who did see it quite enjoyed it too, giving&nbsp;
the movie a respectable audience score of 72%.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.400
Steven Spielberg decided to take on The BFG&nbsp;
in 2016 under the Disney banner. Spielberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.680
Dahl, and Disney. Sounds like&nbsp;
the perfect combo, right? Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.640
the result of this historic pairing was... just&nbsp;
kind of okay. The critical consensus was that:

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.280
"The BFG minimizes the darker elements&nbsp;
of Roald Dahl's classic in favor of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.880
resolutely good-natured, visually stunning, and&nbsp;
largely successful family-friendly adventure."

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.600
In other words, it's Disney at its safest. Other&nbsp;
critics were more critical, with one writing:

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.920
"Spielberg has become a hit-and-miss filmmaker,&nbsp;
and this is closer to a 'miss' than a 'hit.'"

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.480
The critics' Tomatometer&nbsp;
score is currently at 74%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.760
while audiences were much less impressed,&nbsp;
giving it a 57%. Who would've thought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.120
combined creative might of Spielberg, Dahl, and&nbsp;
Disney could produce something so darn mediocre?

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.520
Roald Dahl cemented his icon&nbsp;
status by writing children's books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.520
but he made his living by writing just&nbsp;
about everything. And among Dahl's more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.680
interesting works is the 1965&nbsp;
suspense thriller 36 Hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.840
which holds the distinction of having been&nbsp;
the first ever film adaptation of his writing.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.520
Set in occupied Germany in 1949, James Garner&nbsp;
plays an amnesiac American military officer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.040
whose last memories were General&nbsp;
Eisenhower's final directives before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.280
the Normandy invasion five years earlier.&nbsp;
But soon, the American officer realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.120
the psychiatrist he thought was trying&nbsp;
to help him is actually out to get him.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.160
36 Hours is based on a 1944 Dahl short&nbsp;
story called "Beware of the Dog," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.080
ably demonstrates just how different&nbsp;
Dahl was during the war — after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.920
the man was a spy and a fighter&nbsp;
pilot. The results are impressive too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.880
on both the page and the big screen, as&nbsp;
36 Hours boasts an 80% critic's score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.480
and a 77% audience score, marking the movie as&nbsp;
the best adaptation of Dahl's adult fiction.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.920
Johnny Depp really could do no wrong in the&nbsp;
mid-2000s. He had just redefined himself from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.720
quirky indie darling actor to quirky A-lister&nbsp;
by starring in Pirates of the Caribbean:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.640
Curse of the Black Pearl. And a year before&nbsp;
the sequel, Dead Man's Chest, came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:36.720
Depp took a detour to Dahl. In 2005, he reunited&nbsp;
with his buddy Tim Burton to play Willy Wonka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.040
in arguably Roald Dahl's most famous&nbsp;
story, Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.920
While it may have seemed counterintuitive at the&nbsp;
time, Burton's cinematic sensibilities toward the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.800
creepy and the spooky were actually the perfect&nbsp;
fit for Roald Dahl's work, which always managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.440
blend the surreal and scary with the silly. And at&nbsp;
the time, you weren't going to get a better actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.840
to play the world-famous but notoriously&nbsp;
reclusive chocolatier than Johnny Depp.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.640
"I'm Augustus Gloop. I love your chocolate."

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.040
"I can see that. So do I. I never&nbsp;
expected to have so much in common."

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.240
The team had their work cut out for them, however,&nbsp;
as whatever they made would inevitably be compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:20.240
to the iconic 1971 film, Willy Wonka and the&nbsp;
Chocolate Factory. Luckily, Burton, Depp and team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:25.360
were up to the task, as Charlie and Chocolate&nbsp;
Factory scored an 83% critics' score — though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.160
moviegoer's didn't find the film nearly so&nbsp;
sweet, giving it a disappointingly rotten 51%.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.040
Danny DeVito is a genuine national&nbsp;
treasure — but you already know that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.600
don't you? Before he reinvented himself as Frank&nbsp;
Reynolds on It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.240
DeVito was actually an acclaimed actor and&nbsp;
filmmaker — and among his most famous films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:49.200
was his 1996 adaptation of Roald&nbsp;
Dahl's Matilda. Released in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.920
Matilda is arguably Roald Dahl's last great&nbsp;
work. It tells the story of a young girl named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.120
Matilda Wormwood who has wicked parents, a cruel&nbsp;
headmistress, and some extraordinary powers.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.480
DeVito cast himself and his then-wife Rhea Perlman&nbsp;
as Matilda's parents, while Mara Wilson of Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:09.920
Doubtfire and Miracle on 34th Street fame earned&nbsp;
the role of the eponymous Matilda. Critics noted:

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.320
"While the movie diverges from Roald Dahl,&nbsp;
it nonetheless captures the book's spirit."

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.360
Matilda also captured an&nbsp;
impressive Tomatometer score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.020
too — 90% from critics and 73% from moviegoers.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.200
Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory is arguably&nbsp;
the most iconic Roald Dahl movie adaption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.400
Heck, it's one of the most beloved&nbsp;
family movies of all time, period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.880
and the movie has charmed viewers of&nbsp;
all ages since it was released in 1971.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.560
Surprisingly, however, one viewer&nbsp;
who wasn't charmed was Roald Dahl.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:41.920
According to a biography of the&nbsp;
author by his friend Donald Sturrock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:45.200
Dahl didn't much care for the music&nbsp;
or the director, and had, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.160
"serious reservations" with&nbsp;
Gene Wilder's performance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.800
the titular Willy Wonka — despite that same&nbsp;
performance having gone down in movie history.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.520
"You get nothing! You lose! Good day, sir!"

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.520
According to Sturrock:

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.640
"Roald eventually came to tolerate the&nbsp;
film. [...] But he never liked it."

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.160
Dahl was mostly alone in his assessment&nbsp;
of Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:11.040
however, which boasts a 90% critics'&nbsp;
score and an 87% moviegoers' score.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:16.160
It took decades for filmmakers to realize&nbsp;
Roald Dahl stories were perfect fodder for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:21.920
stop-motion animation. Finally, however, 1996&nbsp;
saw the release of James and the Giant Peach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:26.960
by far one of the best Roald Dahl adaptations.&nbsp;
Nine years before Tim Burton directed Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.800
and the Chocolate Factory, he produced&nbsp;
James and the Giant Peach by employing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.520
the same stop-motion techniques as the iconic&nbsp;
stop-motion film The Nightmare Before Christmas.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.840
Helmed by Nightmare director Henry Selick,&nbsp;
James and the Giant Peach was praised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.640
critics, with Rotten Tomatoes' consensus reading:

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:45.760
"The arresting and dynamic&nbsp;
visuals, offbeat details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.840
and light-as-air storytelling make James and&nbsp;
the Giant Peach solid family entertainment."

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.400
Critics gave the movie an&nbsp;
impressive 91% Tomatometer score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:59.120
though moviegoers were much less&nbsp;
impressed, with a decidedly unripened 66%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:02.560
score. Moviegoers' apathy with James&nbsp;
and the Giant Peach was reflected in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.440
the film's box office too, which bombed&nbsp;
hard with a meager $28 million worldwide.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:12.160
When it comes to fantasy stories that&nbsp;
entertain children and adults alike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.800
few can match the talent of Roald Dahl. One&nbsp;
who certainly can, however, is Jim Henson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.040
creator of Sesame Street and The Muppets.&nbsp;
Dahl's talent for storytelling and Henson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.200
creative prowess as a producer came&nbsp;
together in 1990's The Witches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.600
and the results were nothing less than magical.&nbsp;
According to Rotten Tomatoes' consensus:

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.880
"The Witches is a dark and witty&nbsp;
movie [that] captures the spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.160
of Roald Dahl's imagination&nbsp;
like few other adaptations."

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.720
Critics also praised Anjelica Huston's, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.200
"deliciously wicked performance" as the Grand&nbsp;
High Witch, as well as Jim Henson's typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.880
brilliant puppetry. All in all, everybody&nbsp;
was on top of their game with this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:51.360
resulting in one of the most impressive and&nbsp;
faithful adaptations of Roald Dahl's work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.360
And it's got the Tomatometer score to prove it,&nbsp;
too, with 93% from critics and 70% from audiences.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:02.640
Fantastic Mr. Fox is a movie truly worthy&nbsp;
of its name. Roald Dahl published the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.920
anthropomorphic adventures of Mr. Fox, Mrs.&nbsp;
Fox, their family and their friends in 1970,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:13.600
and in 2009, Wes Anderson adapted the story for&nbsp;
his first venture into stop-motion animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.240
While Fantastic Mr. Fox's brisk 96 pages&nbsp;
are as delightfully Dahl as it gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.320
the story isn't quite his best-known.&nbsp;
Nonetheless, the quirky whimsy of Dahl's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:27.200
lesser-appreciated story matches perfectly&nbsp;
with Anderson's cinematic sensibilities.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.400
"By the way, you look unbelievably&nbsp;
beautiful tonight. You're practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.480
glowing. Maybe it's the light."

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:37.360
While the two acclaimed artists never met,&nbsp;
Anderson clearly shares a creative kinship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.360
with Dahl, and both managed to cultivate&nbsp;
a similar sense of childlike spirit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:46.000
their works — and that spirit comes together&nbsp;
perfectly with Mr. Fox. Critics seem to agree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:50.720
too, and with a 93% critics'&nbsp;
score and 85% audience score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:55.120
Fantastic Mr. Fox ranks as the&nbsp;
finest Roald Dahl adaptation to date.

